For all sufficiently large primes $p$, uniformly in $l\ge1$, define  egin{align*} M_{l,p}&=\sum_{\substack{S\subseteq[w]\|S|\le t+1}} p^{-|S|\delta/2}
u_{l,p,S},\ \mu_{l,p}&= rac{M_{l,p}}{M_{l,p}(1)},\ \eps_{l,p}&= rac{1}{M_{l,p}(1)} \sum_{|S|=t+1}p^{-(t+1)\delta/2}
u_{l,p,S}. \end{align*} Then $\mu_{l,p}$ is a reflection-positive probability law and $0\le\eps_{l,p}\le\mu_{l,p}$. Its exceptional mass satisfies \[ \eta_{l,p}:=\eps_{l,p}(1)\le p^{-4}. \] The probability law \[ \mu_{l,p}^{\circ} = rac{\mu_{l,p}-\eps_{l,p}}{1-\eta_{l,p}} \] has only admissible increments $z_{v_{j+1}}-z_{v_j}$ along the designated cycle. Every component and both measures $\mu_{l,p}$, $\eps_{l,p}$ are invariant under simultaneous translation of all labels. Every positive-mass component or subsum therefore has uniform single-slot marginals after normalization. In either $\mu_{l,p}$ or $\mu_{l,p}^{\circ}$, the total probability of components with $S
e\varnothing$ is $O(p^{-\delta/2})$.
